  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "An exploratory sequential mixed-methods design analyzed 660 app reviews, two focus groups, and a survey of 505 urban residents using partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM) to understand crime-reporting app usage. The study identified perceived value as a formative higher-order construct driven by safety, anonymity, autonomy, and camaraderie. This construct served as the strongest predictor of usage intent, explaining 75% of the variance in behavioral intention and 51% of actual app use.",
      "why": "App designers can now prioritize specific social and psychological drivers—specifically safety, privacy controls, and community camaraderie—over traditional usability metrics like effort expectancy when attempting to retain users on civic reporting platforms."
    }
